<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aymara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aymara people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aymara are an Indigenous people of the Andean altiplano around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Chile, speaking the Aymara language. After the collapse of Tiwanaku several Aymara kingdoms, such as the Lupaca, Colla and Pacajes, ruled the region until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest in the 15th century. Under Spanish rule they paid tribute and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, and today they number about two million.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aymara.jpg" alt="Aymara" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michelle Bachelet · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aimaras_Pozo_Almonte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
